bly doing high content  imaging um this is often done with  multi-world plates so like a 96 well  plate so you can image lots of samples  one after another extremely quickly  and sometimes do multiple dimensions and  multiple channels so yeah  it's most useful for these high  throughput applications  so you can maximize the data collection  for a better statistical analysis so  you've got examples such as in drug  discovery  imaging dense tissue with high mag  stitching  and lab on a chip imaging which is also  pretty interesting so like the other  applications i've spoken about  we want to go fast we want x  y and z dimensional in uh information  here you do want to get  that depth information in your samples  as well um  but you also want to add potentially  multiple channels like in the  multi-color fluorescence image you can  see on the right here  so we then have the time required um  with with uh say you're using a multiple  plate you then have the time required to  move to the next well  as well and to take your next set of  images so speed really in this case  means  minimizing downtime so it's another  definition of speed but also an  incredibly important one  because with high content imaging with  so much data that you want to acquire  the faster and the larger the amount of  data that can be captured the better  so a high frame rate is a given because  you can capture your x y and z  information faster but a large large  field of view is also important because  you can maximize the amount of data  acquired in a single acquisition  so you you know you don't want to waste  time  acquiring extra images to tile and  stitch if you don't have to  and that's where the kinetics can make a  big difference to efficiency and  throughput  in high content imaging  so to summarize what we've presented in  this webinar today  high speed imaging it's a multi-faceted  challenge and it will always involve  some amount of compromise and and in  many applications  speed alone is never the only concern  and as we just saw different  applications need speed for different  reasons  and there will always almost always be  an additional benefit  and whether that's the additional field  of view that's needed or the sensitivity  that's needed or the resolution that's  needed  it's very rare that speed is needed in a  vacuum  and when we created the kinetics  we created it specifically to address  these challenges with the unique  combination of specifications  that feature the extremely high frame  rate the massive field of view  and the back illuminated low read noise  sensitivity and  on top of that the ability to use the  three different modes on the camera  to further optimize based on  experimental requirements  so thank you very much from both me  and ratchet we're going to be around for  the next 10 minutes or so to take  questions  so again thank you and please ask away  okay some of those questions are rolling  in um  i guess we can get uh dan do you want to  just kind of go through the questions  and we can  kind of answer them as we go uh yeah  absolutely  um so yeah let's start with  a couple of quick ones just to get  um get some quick answers for you guys  um  what is the size of the camera pixels  and the kinetics has 6.5 by 6.5 micron  pixels  um another quick one um for you ratchet  where is the data stored on the camera  or streamed  uh it is streamed straight to the pc  um another one uh when we have  on the micromanager software actually  it's a micromanager is a free software  to control your camera  and does this software also allow you to  do tile and stitch operations  uh now i believe that it does ratchet um  do you have uh  other information i've i've definitely  seen thailand stitch applications been  done with um  using micromanager before um i don't  know the exact  uh way you'd have to do it without a  little bit of reading um  but i'm pretty sure i have seen it uh  ratchet yeah as long as you  as long as you have the the the hardware  that goes into micromanager that will  allow you to do that tile and stitching  as well  then then yes it should allow you to do  that  perfect um so a bit of um  a bit more of a complex question which  i'm  also going to send your way ratchet um  does  so so an 8-bit mode does an 8-bit mode  have enough  dynamic range for for for real imaging  uh i think the short answer is yes but  it it also depends  on um you know how the quantification  happens so if you take you know if we  keep in mind  eight bits is roughly you know 256  different  intensities of light there uh you want  to be able to have enough  intensity resolution so it's not that  each one of those intensity levels  is a extremely large number an extremely  large amount of signal  and so there is a bit of a trade-off  there with the maximum amount of signal  that you're able to measure  is reduced but i think in terms of does  it have enough dynamic range  yes plus if you're using the 8-bit mode  you're going  extremely fast so the amount of flight  you would have per frame is  is also reduced yeah  exactly and i've seen the same thing  when i've been out in labs with the  camera  myself it's when you're going extremely  fast obviously your exposure times are  low the amount of light you're acquiring  is low  um so you're only actually using that  very bottom  end of the um of the dynamic range  anyway which is perfectly within  the realm of of where we have our 8-bit  mode um  so yeah it has been used successfully  quantitatively as well  another quick one and how compatible  with kinetics with  labview packages that's one for you  ratchet  uh it's compatible we have a telden  photometrics labview adapter on our  website that  you can go download now it's freely  available  so it should be should be fully uh you  know useful  usable in labview perfect  um so an application specific question  here  um so in terms of single molecule  imaging at high speeds what is the most  important  or limiting factor in modern scmos  cameras  um for example is it read noise quantum  efficiency pixel size  um that's a really great question um  because  it's entirely dependent on  on the application um as with as with  all these things  whenever i visit um a lab with the  camera it's always okay  so you're doing this but what exactly  specifically are you doing  um you know for some people uh doing  single molecule imaging their the field  of view is very important because they  want to maximize the number of molecules  they're looking at  and for some they don't care about field  of view at all and they'll happily  shrink right down to look at say a  strand of dna that they want to look at  individual fluorophores landing on and  in which case they can go super small  and then they can boost their  uh their frame rates right up in that  way um  generally speaking what i what i always  see with single molecule imaging  is um it is low photon count  um is very low signals so generally  speaking  um what we've seen to make the biggest  difference  in terms of single molecule imaging is  particularly with  the prime95b scmos camera which is  another one of our cameras  has been increasing sensitivity through  the 95  quantum efficiency the back illuminated  sensor but also the larger pixel size  as this was typically done with emc  cameras in the past  um which had a larger pixel and the fact  that the prime95v has a larger pixel and  is a back illuminated cmos  we often found made one of the biggest  differences for single molecule imaging  and caused  um most people we've visited to change  from emc ccd to scmos and to take  advantage of the larger field of view or  the larger speed  or the increased resolution  another one for you ratchet uh so how do  you handle the field of view limitations  of microscope optics  uh so what i would say is uh the the  microscope  that dan was using today is a nikon ti2  which  the the optics on that stand have  allowed us to use the full  field of view of the of the kinetics  camera  but otherwise i would say that having  the ability to region down  and still have significantly improved  frame rates over  you know those typical scmos cameras is  a pretty good advantage there  it's always a little bit more helpful to  have more area than you need and  to be able to reduce it than have the  opposite problem of not having enough  area  yeah absolutely so what  what mount does the camera come with  then to access the full sensor size  so the camera comes with uh it comes  with an f-mount  but the way that we've designed the  camera the mounts are actually swappable  so you're able to remove that f-mount  and  screw on a t-mount which is just a you  know a very large c-mount if you will  and then if you if you have a microscope  stand that doesn't necessarily have an  f-mount or t-mount  compatible adapter it does also come  with a c-mount front which you can then  use  um on the kinetics you know with with  the with the  with the condition that you know you'll  have to reduce the the effective field  of view down to you know 22 millimeters  at the most  god thanks ratchet um so last question  i'm i'm afraid we're  um short of time so it's just one more  question i'll be able to answer but  um yeah i will make sure to follow up  with everyone who has  asked questions myself and ratchet will  send you an email  with answers to your questions after the  broadcast don't worry your questions  will be answered  um the last one that i just want to  address in is where can i get  information about these cameras um you  can get information about all of our  cameras  on our website photometrics.com if you  go to the products drop down at the top  you'll see the kinetics right at the top  of it that will give you  a full breakdown of all the  specifications  applications information and you know  other cool stuff in there you can use  like a a calculator that we've got that  lets you set for example  a speed that you would like to achieve  and it will tell you the region size  that you should be using for that  and the other way around if you set a  region size it will tell you the speed  so some cool resources on there but if  you just go to  your product section our website you can  find out even more about this camera  and our others so thank you again  everyone for attending um i will follow  up with the rest of you who have asked  questions  after the broadcast um ratchet do you  want to  sign off as well yeah absolutely thank  you thank you all for taking the time to  to attend and uh you know glad that  we're glad that you could be here and  hopefully hopefully this was  helpful and you know the the kinetics is  something that  that is interesting to you  cool thank you everyone thank you  everyone thanks everyone  you
 

